submcu: answer the no-interrupt-pending case from two bytes

SM_HandleInterrupt walks nine arms, each three loads and three tests deep, and
in the common running state -- interrupts enabled, nothing pending -- every arm
fails and the walk was for nothing. Eight of the nine arms require a bit in
INT_REQUEST; the collision arm alone keys on COLLISION bits 7:6. So
INT_REQUEST == 0 together with the collision test failing implies the whole
ladder falls through with no side effects, and those two loads can answer the
common case before the walk starts.

This is patch 0005's trick applied to the sub-MCU, and it matters for the same
reason: the check runs once per sub-MCU instruction, which the mk2 steps 1.25
times for every main-MCU instruction.

Equivalence is proven the same way as 0005: submcu.cpp compiled twice into one
program, upstream in one namespace and this patch in the other, driven over
every reachable (INT_REQUEST, COLLISION, ENABLE, CTRL, cts, sr) combination and
required to agree on every scalar and every outward call. See
patches/tests/mcu/sm_interrupt_scan_equivalence.cpp, and its mutants.

--- a/src/backend/submcu.cpp
+++ b/src/backend/submcu.cpp
@@ -1314,7 +1314,17 @@
 {
     if (sm.sr & SM_STATUS_I)
         return;
-    
+
+    // Nine arms follow. Eight of them require a bit in INT_REQUEST and the
+    // collision arm alone keys on COLLISION bits 7:6, so in the common running
+    // state -- interrupts enabled, nothing pending -- every arm fails and the
+    // ladder walks some twenty-seven loads to conclude nothing. Answer that
+    // case from the two bytes it actually depends on. This runs once per
+    // sub-MCU instruction, 1.25 times per main-MCU instruction on an mk2.
+    if (sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_INT_REQUEST] == 0
+        && (sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_COLLISION] & 0xc0) != 0xc0)
+        return;
+
     if ((sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_UART1_CTRL] & 0x8) != 0
         && (sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_INT_ENABLE] & 0x80) != 0
         && (sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_INT_REQUEST] & 0x80) != 0)
